    \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043c\u0435\u0440\u0430 \u0434\u043b\u044f \u043c\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0434\u044b\u0445
    \u0436\u0435\u043d\u0449\u0438\u043d \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0434\u043e\u043b\u0436\u0430\u0435\u0442
    \u0436\u0438\u0442\u044c."
  shortBioEn: "Mariam Mirzahani is an Iranian and American mathematician, the only woman and first Iranian to be awarded the Fields Prize (2014) for her outstanding contribution to the geometry of the Roman surfaces and the theory of their modules. Her life is a story of amazing talent, overcoming and tragic early care, but her work continues to inspire scientists around the world.\n\nEarly years and the way to mathematics, Mariam Mirzahani was born on 3 or 12 May 1977 in Tehran, Iran, and was the fourth child in the family of an electric engineer, Ahmad Mirzahani and Zahra Hagigi. From an early age, Mariam became involved in reading and dreaming of becoming a writer.At Farzanegan School, which was intended for gifted girls, her journey was not easy: the first teacher thought she had no math skills; however, the change of teacher and support of her friend Roy Beheshti helped her not only to overcome doubts, but also to achieve outstanding success; already in her high school years, she began researching, and her first scientific work, written in co-authorship, was devoted to the theory of graphs.\n\nIn 1994 and 1995, Mariam was the first Iranian schoolgirl to win the gold medals at the International Mathematical Olympics, and in 1995 she achieved all the goals with a maximum score, and her talent was also evident in the difficult task of drawing the graphs, for which she received a symbolic $10 prize from her mentor.In 1999, she graduated from the Sharif University of Technology in Tehran, with a bachelor's degree in mathematics, and in the same year moved to the United States to continue her education, and in 2004, at Harvard University, under the leadership of Curtis McMullen, she defended a Ph.D. dissertation, which was immediately recognized as innovative.\n\nThe scientific career and main achievements of Mirzahani were at the intersection of the geometry of Lobachevsky, Taehmüller theory, ergodic theory and symlexic geometry, and its work changed the approach to the study of the Roman surfaces and the areas of their modules. Key milestones of her career: 2004 - 2008: Research Fellow at the Clay Institute and Associate Professor at Princeton University.Since 2008: Professor of Mathematics at Stanford University, 2014: She was awarded the Fields Prize, the most prestigious award in mathematics awarded by a scientist up to the age of 40, and was awarded the award for \"the outstanding contribution to the dynamics and geometry of the Roman surfaces and the theory of their modules.\" In addition to the Fields Prize, she was awarded the Blumenthal Award (2009), the Ruth Sutter Award (2013) and the Clay Institute Award (2014).She was elected a member of the National Academy of Sciences in 2016.\n\nThe main scientific results of Mirzahani are known for two fundamental achievements: 1. The formula for the size of the modules: In its thesis, she found an elegant recursive formula for calculating the size of the modules of the rhman surfaces.It has solved the problem of calculating the number of simple closed geodetic lines on hyperbolic surfaces, and its work has offered new evidence of the famous Maxim Kontevich theorem relevant to quantum field theory. 2. The magic wand theory: In working with Alex Eskin (2013), it has proved the fundamental theory of the dynamics of the pool ball in the polygon.Their work, which is about 200 pages long, has solved a problem that mathematicians have been struggling on for over a century, and has been so powerful that it has received the unofficial title \"Theory of Magic Finger.\" It has solved many previously inaccessible problems by linking geometry, topology and dynamic systems.\n\n* Mariam's personal life and tragic care was married to a scientist of Czech origin, Ian Wondrak, and they had an Anahita daughter. In 2013, she was diagnosed with breast cancer. Despite her illness, she continued to work and even published a joint article with her husband while she was undergoing treatment. Mariam Mirzahani died on July 14, 2017, at the age of 40, of the effects of metastatic cancer at Stanford University Medical Center.Her departure was a huge loss to the world's scientific community, but her legacy as a genius mathematician and an inspiring example for young women continues to live."
  interestingFactsRu: "\u0416\u0438\u0437\u043d\u044c \u041c\u0430\u0440\u0438\u0430\u043c \u041c\u0438\u0440\u0437\u0430\u0445\u0430\u043d\u0438
